# SMB - Installation and Configuration on Linux

## Introduction

**SMB (Server Message Block)** is a network protocol used to share files, directories, printers, and other resources over a network.

On Linux, the most common implementation of SMB is **Samba**.

The main Samba service is:

```text
smbd

The main configuration file is:

/etc/samba/smb.conf

Modern SMB primarily uses:

TCP/445

This guide explains how to install and configure a basic authenticated SMB server on Linux.

1. Lab Environment

For this guide, we will use the following configuration:

Parameter	Value
Server IP	192.168.1.50
Share name	share
Share directory	/srv/samba/share
Linux group	smbusers
SMB user	smbuser
SMB port	TCP/445

The final architecture will look like this:

                    SMB CLIENT
                        |
                        | TCP/445
                        |
                        v
                +---------------+
                |    Samba      |
                |     smbd      |
                +---------------+
                        |
                        v
              /srv/samba/share
2. Installing Samba

Update the package repository:

sudo apt update

Install Samba and the SMB client:

sudo apt install samba smbclient

The samba package provides the server components, while smbclient provides command-line tools for interacting with SMB servers.

Check the installed Samba version:

smbd --version

Check the Samba service:

systemctl status smbd
3. Starting the Samba Service

Enable Samba so that it starts automatically at boot:

sudo systemctl enable smbd

Start the service:

sudo systemctl start smbd

Alternatively, both operations can be performed with:

sudo systemctl enable --now smbd

Check that Samba is running:

systemctl is-active smbd

Expected output:

active
4. Creating a Linux Group

Create a dedicated Linux group for users who are allowed to access the SMB share:

sudo groupadd smbusers

Verify the group:

getent group smbusers
5. Creating a Linux User

Create the Linux user:

sudo adduser smbuser

Add the user to the smbusers group:

sudo usermod -aG smbusers smbuser

Verify the user's groups:

id smbuser

Example:

uid=1001(smbuser) gid=1001(smbuser) groups=1001(smbuser),1002(smbusers)

At this point, smbuser is a normal Linux user.

The user must also be added to Samba's authentication database.

6. Creating the Shared Directory

Create the directory that will be exposed through SMB:

sudo mkdir -p /srv/samba/share

Set the owner to root and the group to smbusers:

sudo chown root:smbusers /srv/samba/share

Set the permissions:

sudo chmod 2770 /srv/samba/share

Check the result:

ls -ld /srv/samba/share

Expected output:

drwxrws--- root smbusers /srv/samba/share
Understanding 2770

The permission value 2770 means:

2    770
|    |
|    +---- rwx rwx ---
|
+--------- setgid

Therefore:

Owner: rwx
Group: rwx
Others: no permissions
setgid: new files and directories inherit the smbusers group

The setgid bit is useful when multiple users need to work with the same shared directory.

7. Creating the Samba Account

A Linux account and a Samba account are related, but Samba maintains its own authentication database.

Add the Linux user to Samba:

sudo smbpasswd -a smbuser

Enter the Samba password when prompted.

Enable the Samba account:

sudo smbpasswd -e smbuser

List Samba users:

sudo pdbedit -L

You should see:

smbuser
8. Backing Up the Samba Configuration

The main Samba configuration file is:

/etc/samba/smb.conf

Before modifying it, create a backup:

sudo cp /etc/samba/smb.conf /etc/samba/smb.conf.backup

Edit the configuration file:

sudo nano /etc/samba/smb.conf
9. Configuring smb.conf

For a simple standalone SMB server, use the following configuration:

[global]
   workgroup = WORKGROUP
   server role = standalone server
   security = user
   map to guest = never
   server min protocol = SMB2

[share]
   path = /srv/samba/share
   browseable = yes
   read only = no
   valid users = @smbusers

The configuration contains two main sections:

[global]

and:

[share]

The [global] section contains server-wide settings.

The [share] section defines the SMB share.

10. Understanding the [global] Section
workgroup
workgroup = WORKGROUP

Defines the Windows workgroup name.

WORKGROUP is commonly used in simple home and laboratory environments.

It does not define the server hostname.

server role
server role = standalone server

Defines the Samba server as an independent server.

This configuration is appropriate when Samba is not part of an Active Directory domain.

security
security = user

Enables user-based authentication.

Clients must authenticate using valid Samba credentials before accessing the share.

map to guest
map to guest = never

Prevents failed authentication attempts from being mapped to a guest account.

This means that users must authenticate with valid credentials.

server min protocol
server min protocol = SMB2

Defines the minimum SMB protocol version accepted by the server.

SMB1 is an old protocol and should generally not be enabled on modern systems unless legacy compatibility is specifically required.

11. Understanding the [share] Section

The following section defines the actual SMB share:

[share]

The name share is the name clients will use when connecting.

For example:

\\192.168.1.50\share

The SMB share name does not have to match the Linux directory name.

path
path = /srv/samba/share

Defines the Linux directory that will be exposed through SMB.

In this example:

/srv/samba/share
browseable
browseable = yes

Allows the share to appear when SMB shares are enumerated.

For example:

smbclient -L //192.168.1.50 -U smbuser

The share should appear as:

share
read only
read only = no

Allows authenticated users to perform write operations.

However, this option does not override Linux filesystem permissions.

Linux permissions still determine whether the user can actually read or modify files.

valid users
valid users = @smbusers

Restricts access to members of the smbusers group.

The @ symbol indicates that smbusers is a group.

For example:

valid users = smbuser

would allow only smbuser.

Whereas:

valid users = @smbusers

allows all members of the group.

12. Testing the Samba Configuration

Before restarting Samba, validate the configuration:

sudo testparm

If the configuration is valid, Samba should report that the configuration loaded successfully.

You can also display the effective configuration:

sudo testparm -s

A useful workflow is:

Edit smb.conf
     |
     v
  testparm
     |
     v
Configuration valid?
     |
     v
Restart smbd

Always run testparm after modifying smb.conf.

13. Restarting Samba

After modifying the configuration:

sudo systemctl restart smbd

Check the service:

systemctl status smbd

Verify that it is active:

systemctl is-active smbd

Expected output:

active
14. Checking TCP Port 445

Modern SMB primarily uses TCP port 445.

Check whether Samba is listening:

sudo ss -lntp | grep :445

Example:

LISTEN 0 50 0.0.0.0:445 0.0.0.0:* users:(("smbd",pid=1234,fd=XX))

This confirms that smbd is listening for SMB connections.

15. Testing SMB Locally

Before testing from another machine, test the server locally.

List the available shares:

smbclient -L localhost -U smbuser

Enter the Samba password when prompted.

You should see something similar to:

Sharename       Type
---------       ----
share           Disk
IPC$            IPC

The important entry is:

share
16. Connecting to the SMB Share

Connect directly to the share:

smbclient //localhost/share -U smbuser

After successful authentication:

smb: \>

You can now interact with the share.

List files:

ls

Create a directory:

mkdir test

Upload a file:

put file.txt

Download a file:

get file.txt

Exit:

exit
17. Testing from Another Linux Machine

From another machine on the same network, enumerate the SMB server:

smbclient -L //192.168.1.50 -U smbuser

Then connect to the share:

smbclient //192.168.1.50/share -U smbuser

If authentication succeeds and the share is accessible, the basic SMB configuration is working.

18. Connecting from Windows

From a Windows client, press:

Win + R

Enter:

\\192.168.1.50\share

Press Enter.

Windows should request credentials.

Use:

Username: smbuser
Password: <Samba password>

The shared directory should then be accessible.

19. Firewall Configuration

Check whether UFW is enabled:

sudo ufw status

If necessary, allow Samba:

sudo ufw allow Samba

Check the firewall rules:

sudo ufw status

For a home server, SMB should preferably be accessible only from the trusted local network.

A firewall should prevent untrusted networks from accessing TCP port 445.

20. Do Not Expose SMB Directly to the Internet

SMB should not normally be exposed directly to the public Internet.

Avoid creating a router port forwarding rule such as:

Internet
   |
   | TCP/445
   v
Samba Server

For remote access, a better architecture is to use a VPN such as WireGuard:

              Internet
                  |
                  |
             WireGuard VPN
                  |
                  v
             Home Network
                  |
                  v
          +---------------+
          | Samba Server  |
          | 192.168.1.50  |
          +---------------+
                  |
               TCP/445

The remote client connects to the VPN first and then accesses the SMB server through the private network.

21. SMB Protocol Versions

SMB has evolved through several versions.

Protocol	Description
SMB1	Legacy protocol
SMB2	Modernized SMB protocol
SMB3	Adds additional features and security capabilities

For a modern Samba installation, SMB1 should generally be avoided.

The following configuration prevents SMB1 negotiation:

server min protocol = SMB2
22. Samba Permissions vs Linux Permissions

One of the most important concepts when working with Samba is that access is controlled by two different permission layers.

             SMB Client
                  |
                  v
        +-------------------+
        | Samba permissions |
        +-------------------+
                  |
                  v
        +-------------------+
        | Linux permissions |
        +-------------------+
                  |
                  v
             Filesystem

Both layers must allow the requested operation.

For example:

read only = no

means Samba allows write operations.

However, if Linux permissions deny the user write access, the operation will still fail.

Check the Linux permissions:

ls -ld /srv/samba/share

Example:

drwxrws--- root smbusers /srv/samba/share

Check the user's group membership:

id smbuser
23. Troubleshooting
Samba service is not running

Check:

systemctl status smbd

If necessary:

sudo systemctl restart smbd
Configuration error

Run:

sudo testparm

Correct any errors reported by the command before restarting Samba.

Port 445 is not listening

Check:

sudo ss -lntp | grep :445

Then check:

systemctl status smbd
Authentication fails

Verify that the Samba user exists:

sudo pdbedit -L

Reset the Samba password if necessary:

sudo smbpasswd smbuser

Make sure the account is enabled:

sudo smbpasswd -e smbuser
User cannot access the share

Check group membership:

id smbuser

Check directory ownership and permissions:

ls -ld /srv/samba/share

Check the permissions of the complete path:

namei -l /srv/samba/share
Share is not visible

List the shares manually:

smbclient -L //192.168.1.50 -U smbuser

If the share appears here but not in a graphical network browser, the problem may be related to network discovery rather than SMB authentication.

You can still access the share directly:

\\192.168.1.50\share
