26. Final Configuration

The final /etc/samba/smb.conf should contain:

[global]
   workgroup = WORKGROUP
   server role = standalone server
   security = user
   map to guest = never
   server min protocol = SMB2

[share]
   path = /srv/samba/share
   browseable = yes
   read only = no
   valid users = @smbusers

The resulting configuration is:

Server
└── Samba (smbd)
    ├── TCP/445
    ├── Authentication
    │   └── smbuser
    │
    └── Share
        └── [share]
            └── /srv/samba/share
Conclusion

A basic authenticated Samba server requires several components to work together:

Samba (smbd) provides the SMB service.
/etc/samba/smb.conf defines the server and its shares.
Samba users provide authentication.
Linux users and groups control filesystem access.
Linux permissions determine what users can actually do with files.
TCP/445 is the main port used by modern SMB.
A firewall should restrict SMB to trusted networks.
WireGuard or another VPN can be used for secure remote access.

The key concept is:

Samba authentication
        +
Samba share permissions
        +
Linux filesystem permissions
        =
Effective access
